/**

 @page tutorials_multigrid_petsc_mixed_dg_poisson Mixed Discontinuous Galerkin for the Poisson equation with PETSc

<!-- Add this one for href links to subitems -->
\tableofcontents


Following the discussion in @ref page_multigrid_numerics_Mixed_Galerkin, we instantiate


Later on, we will use a value unknowns_per_cell_dof. This is 


           unknowns_per_cell_dof = 3

In the code, we expect the user to specify the number of unknowns equired
per cell plus the polynomial order:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
solver = petsc.solvers.MixedPoisson("MP",
                                    polynomial_degree = 2,
                                    unknowns_per_cell_dof = 3,
                                    [...]
                                    )
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

The code takes this information, runs through the mesh and allocates (from
PETSc) @f$ 3 \cdot (2+1)^d @f$ unknown indices. That means, so far, we are
trying to solve a system 

@f$ A x = b @f$

where x has @f$ 3 |\mathbf{C}| \cdot (p+1)^d @f$ entries. This is not the 
end of the story. Only a start. 



In the code, we expect the user to specify the number of unknowns equired
per cell plus the polynomial order:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
solver = petsc.solvers.MixedDiscontinuousGalerkinDiscretisation("Poisson",
                                                           args.degree,
                                                           unknowns_per_cell_dof = 3,
                                                           args.meshsize,
                                                           args.meshsize,
                                                           )
solver = petsc.solvers.MixedPoisson("MP",
                                    polynomial_degree = 2,
                                    unknowns_per_cell_dof = 3,
                                    [...]
                                    )
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~




 */
